          Same build. Same price sheet. The city pages own the city. This page
          owns the front desk that is with a patient.
        </p>

        <h2 class="heading-lg measure">Dental practices: the hygiene-recall and new-patient call pattern</h2>
        <p>
          Two calls show up while the desk is with a patient in the chair. A
          new patient wants a first visit. A hygiene-recall caller wants the
          next cleaning time. Both are scheduling calls. Neither one is a
          request for advice, and neither one waits until the chair is empty.
        </p>
        <p>
          The setup asks the questions you approved and books a real open slot,
          or it takes the message you defined. It does not decide that a
          patient is due. It does not invent a recall interval. It does not
          quote a production number, because we do not have one we are willing
          to print. A caller who describes pain, swelling, or a broken tooth
          does not get an interpretation. That call follows the escalation
          list you wrote. {clinicalBoundary}
        </p>
        <p>
          A dental office and a medical office buy the same build. The
          difference is the list you write: visit types, block lengths, and
          who gets the call that is not a booking. We do not publish a second
          script for a second profession.
        </p>

        <h2 class="heading-lg measure">After-hours and weekend patient calls</h2>
        <p>
          Nights and weekends are not a second product and not a second rate.
          The same rules run after the desk goes home. A Saturday caller
          usually wants a time, or they want a person. The setup books the
          next real open slot, or it follows the escalation list you wrote.
        </p>
        <p>
          {clinicalBoundary} After hours, that sentence is the whole policy.
          The setup does not invent an on-call rotation. It does not tell the
          caller a nurse is on the way. It does not tell the caller a provider
          will call back unless you wrote that rule and named the person. If
          you want one changeover, one night, or one weekend path treated
          differently, you write that rule. Until you do, every after-hours
          call uses the same list.
        </p>

        <h2 class="heading-lg measure">Bilingual patient callers</h2>
        <p>
          Here is a hiring fact, not a caller count: employers across dental,
          medical, and food processing in northwest Arkansas have posted
          front-desk listings that specifically ask for Spanish-speaking
          ability. We are not publishing a count of those ads, and we are not
          publishing a share of patient callers who prefer Spanish. We did not
          measure either one.
        </p>
        <p>
          If your callers include people who prefer Spanish, a setup that only
          works in English is not answering for the calls those ads exist to
          cover. What the build can and cannot do in Spanish, today, is
          something we demonstrate on the assessment call rather than promise
          in print. The questions stay the ones you approved. The language
          does not add a clinical question, and it does not relax the limit
          on advice.
        </p>

        <h2 class="heading-lg measure">Pricing for a medical or dental office</h2>
        <p>
          One published price sheet, the same everywhere in Arkansas. A solo
